# 14. The development roadmap: what to build first

The biggest risk in SARVAGYA is attempting to build every module at once. A large feature list is not the same as a reliable product.

I would develop it in six stages.

## 01

### Foundation and stability

First priority

- Reliable login and session management.
- Consistent API structure.
- Working database and migrations.
- Frontend/backend error handling.
- WebSocket reconnection and streaming.
- Secure API-key management.
- Logging and health checks.

Exit condition: the application can be restarted, users can reconnect, and core data is not lost.

## 02

### Intelligence and research

- Model Gateway for Gemini and other configured providers.
- AI Companion streaming.
- Web Navigator with source links.
- Document upload and analysis.
- Shared tool interface.
- Model fallback and usage tracking.

Exit condition: real requests produce useful responses, sources are traceable, and provider failures are handled correctly.

## 03

### Memory and integration

- User-approved memory.
- Project-specific context.
- Knowledge indexing and retrieval.
- Universal Command Center.
- Shared artifacts and workspace.
- Cross-module navigation.

Exit condition: users can continue projects and reuse results across modules.

## 04

### Agents and automation

- Durable background-job queue.
- Task planning and tool execution.
- Scheduling and notifications.
- Approval flows.
- Retry, cancellation and audit history.

Exit condition: multi-step tasks survive page refreshes and recover safely from common failures.

## 05

### Complete the product modules

- Student Hub.
- Creator Studio.
- Interactive Labs.
- Maps & Weather.
- Space & Transit.
- Job & Business.
- Stock Market.
- Developer Hub.
- Plugin Center.

Exit condition: each feature uses real data or real functionality and has meaningful error states.

## 06

### Evolution and scale

- Evaluation benchmarks.
- Cost and performance optimization.
- Improved monitoring.
- Controlled software upgrades.
- Load and security testing.
- Mobile/PWA improvements.
- More providers and integrations.

Exit condition: updates are measured, tested, reversible and monitored in production.

These stages are ordered by dependency, not by a promised delivery schedule. Their duration depends on the existing codebase, available resources, provider access and testing results.

## What should be postponed?

Do not start with unrestricted autonomous coding, dozens of model providers, a giant plugin marketplace, continuous model training, or a hundred separate AI tools.

First make the core reliable. Then add capabilities that reuse that foundation.

# 15. How to know whether each feature is genuinely complete

Every module needs an explicit definition of done.

For example, a Web Navigator is not finished merely because it has a search box. It is finished when its expected workflow works from beginning to end.

| Module         | Minimum proof of completion                               |
| -------------- | --------------------------------------------------------- |
| AI Companion   | Real provider response, streaming, errors and history     |
| Web Navigator  | Search, relevant retrieval, source links and timestamps   |
| Memory         | Save, retrieve, correct and delete with access controls   |
| Student Hub    | Persisted notes, working tests and accurate progress      |
| Creator Studio | Real supported generation or editing workflow             |
| AI Agents      | Durable jobs, permissions, retries and completion records |
| Stock Market   | Clearly sourced data with accurate freshness labels       |
| Plugin Center  | Installation, permission checks, execution and revocation |
| Evolution HUD  | Actual system metrics and recorded evaluation results     |
| Settings       | Changes persist and are enforced by the backend           |

For each module, test normal operation, invalid input, provider failure, lost connection, missing permission, repeated requests and data recovery.

A feature that looks finished but cannot pass these tests should be considered a prototype, not a production-ready capability.

